{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nRecover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n\nRule:\n- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n\nKey insight:\nThe hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b2bc3ffd\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row\nthat acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects\nabove that stripe.\n\nRule\nClear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward\nby the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave\nthe full-width stripe unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nEach object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a\nfixed baseline, not a moving object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n<description>\nSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.\n- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.\n\nRule:\n- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.\n- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.\n- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).\n- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.\n\nKey insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e39e9282\" lines=\"73-81\">\n<description>\nProject maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, solid 3x3 grey and/or pink squares, and maroon marker pixels or segments directly touching one side of exactly one square.\nRule\nGrey squares are erased; their adjacent maroon markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell inside the grey square. Pink squares stay pink; their adjacent maroon markers disappear and stamp the corresponding cell on the square's middle row or middle column.\nKey insight\nThe side touched by a marker determines the projection: grey uses the near edge, while pink uses the centerline, preserving the marker's coordinate along that side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ad8a7c0\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nConnect the innermost mirrored marker pair(s) by filling their horizontal span.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Each foreground row contains exactly two foreground pixels mirrored across the vertical center.\n\nRule\n- Find the foreground row pair(s) with the smallest horizontal gap between the two markers.\n- Fill every pixel between those two markers in each selected row with the foreground color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Wider marker pairs are context; the closest marker pair(s) show the unresolved horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aa62e3f4\" lines=\"18-35\">\n<description>\nReplace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.\n- The outline is the unique largest foreground color.\n- One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color\n  is the unique smallest non-outline color.\n\nRule:\n- Erase all original foreground pixels to background.\n- Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the\n  marker color.\n- Keep the grid shape unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the\nlargest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"470c91de\" lines=\"48-65\">\n<description>\nMove each teal-cornered filled rectangle one cell diagonally toward its teal corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- Dominant background surrounds several filled rectangular colored objects.\n- Each object has exactly one teal control pixel replacing one corner cell of\n  its rectangle.\n\nRule:\n- Treat the teal pixel as the missing corner of its colored rectangle.\n- Erase each original rectangle footprint, including the teal pixel.\n- Repaint the completed rectangle shifted one cell diagonally in the direction\n  of that teal corner.\n\nKey insight:\n- The control pixel's corner position gives both the missing cell to restore\n  and the diagonal motion vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.\n- Write black everywhere else in the block.\n\nKey insight\nEach pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac0c2ac3\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nFill each centered square ring with the color of the marker at that ring distance.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an odd square grid with a dominant background color and one\nnon-background marker for every Chebyshev distance from the grid center.\nRule\nThe output replaces the background with concentric square rings centered on\nthe middle cell; every cell at distance d from the center takes the color of\nthe unique marker whose distance from the center is d.\nKey insight\nThe scattered markers are not objects to move; their Chebyshev distances\nform a palette for the centered square rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"269e22fb\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nComplete a visible crop into the shared 20x20 binary motif in its matching orientation.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a two-color rectangular crop of one rotation/reflection of the same 20x20 motif.\n- One color is the motif foreground and the other color is the motif background.\n\nRule:\n- Try each input color as the foreground mask.\n- Match that mask against every possible window in every dihedral orientation of the motif.\n- Repaint the uniquely matched full 20x20 motif with the matched foreground/background colors.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
